import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a5 as r}from"./chunks/framework.Bj-lFevX.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Merger Reference","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/mergers.md","filePath":"reference/mergers.md"}'),s={name:"reference/mergers.md"};function i(n,e,d,h,c,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="merger-reference" tabindex="-1">Merger Reference <a class="header-anchor" href="#merger-reference" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Merger Reference&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page is generated automatically from the <code>swagger-php</code> sources.</p><p>For improvements head over to <a href="https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a> and create a PR 😉</p><p>Mergers decide when two attributes in one collection, such as two schemas or two operations, are the same one, and what the one that survives looks like. <code>Augmenter\\Merge</code> runs them over the <code>Specification</code>&#39;s collections, so each key reaches the compiler once. The <a href="/swagger-php/reference/augmenters.html">Augmenters reference</a> says when it runs.</p><p>Mergers are tried in registration order, and the first to claim a type handles it. The defaults are listed in that order. <code>Merge\\LastWins</code> claims every type, so a merger you add goes ahead of it. The <a href="/swagger-php/guide/extension-points.html#mergers">extension points guide</a> shows how to write and register one.</p><h2 id="default-mergers" tabindex="-1">Default Mergers <a class="header-anchor" href="#default-mergers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Default Mergers&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="tags" tabindex="-1"><a href="https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/Tags.php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tags</a> <a class="header-anchor" href="#tags" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;[Tags](https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/Tags.php)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Folds two tags with the same name field by field, instead of keeping one of them whole.</p><p>One of the Builder&#39;s default mergers, registered ahead of the catch-all: a tag is declared once with its description and then named on every method or class that uses it, and each of those is a declaration too.</p><p>What only one of them sets is always taken, so a tag that is only a name adds nothing and is not reported. What both set differently is decided by the <code>Mode</code> it is constructed with, <code>Mode::Last</code> by default, and reported. <code>NonOverlapping</code> keeps the earlier&#39;s value, as <code>First</code> does. <code>attachables</code> is a union, and <code>x</code> merges key by key under the same mode.</p><p>The survivor is a new tag, a copy of the earlier with the later folded in. It keeps the earlier&#39;s reflector, source location and <code>meta</code>.</p><h4 id="config-settings" tabindex="-1">Config settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#config-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Config settings&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li><strong>mode</strong> : <code>OpenApi\\Merge\\Mode|callable</code> · default: <code>Mode::Last</code><br> How two attributes with the same identity are treated where they overlap: a <code>Mode</code>, or a callable that receives the earlier and the later and returns one.</li></ul><h3 id="lastwins" tabindex="-1"><a href="https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/LastWins.php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LastWins</a> <a class="header-anchor" href="#lastwins" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;[LastWins](https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/LastWins.php)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The catch-all merger: one entry per key survives, the later one, and the author is told.</p><p>It reduces rather than folds: two entries in, one out, so the compiler never sees a collision and its own accidental rules stop deciding anything. Combining fields from both attributes is a type-specific merger&#39;s job, registered ahead of this one.</p><p>It claims every type, and keys each by the identity all shipped mergers share.</p><h2 id="opt-in-mergers" tabindex="-1">Opt-in Mergers <a class="header-anchor" href="#opt-in-mergers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Opt-in Mergers&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="operations" tabindex="-1"><a href="https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/Operations.php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Operations</a> <a class="header-anchor" href="#operations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;[Operations](https://github.com/zircote/swagger-php/tree/master/src/Merge/Operations.php)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Folds two operations for the same path and method field by field, instead of keeping one of them whole.</p><p>Opt-in: it is not one of the Builder&#39;s default mergers. Register it ahead of the catch-all, <code>withMergers(fn ($mergers) =&gt; $mergers-&gt;insert(new Merge\\Operations(), Merge\\LastWins::class))</code>, when one operation is described in more than one place, such as a route table contributed through <code>withSpecification()</code> and an attribute adding responses to it.</p><p>Identity is the shared one: path and method, or webhook and method. Registering the fold changes how two operations combine, not which two are the same.</p><p>What only one of them sets is always taken. What both set is decided by the <code>Mode</code> it is constructed with, <code>Mode::Last</code> by default: fold into the later, fold into the earlier, or take only what does not overlap. The mode can also be a callable that receives both and returns one, so the choice can follow a mark a producer left with <code>setMeta()</code> rather than the order they arrive in. A field both set differently is reported, whichever value is kept. One they set the same is not.</p><ul><li>Parameters are keyed by name and location, and responses by status code. Under <code>First</code> and <code>Last</code>, a parameter or response both describe is folded one level deep, a parameter&#39;s <code>schema</code> included, so a route&#39;s <code>pattern</code> survives an attribute&#39;s <code>type</code>. Under <code>NonOverlapping</code> it is the earlier&#39;s, whole.</li><li><code>requestBody</code>, <code>externalDocs</code>, <code>security</code>, <code>servers</code> and <code>callbacks</code> are each one decision, taken whole from whichever operation the mode keeps. An explicit <code>security: []</code> is set, and opts out.</li><li><code>tags</code> and <code>attachables</code> are unions, and <code>x</code> merges key by key under the same mode.</li></ul><p>The survivor is a new operation, a shallow copy of the earlier with the later folded in, so neither operation is changed. What it takes whole, such as a response or a request body, is the contribution&#39;s own object, and the augmenters that run later change it in place, as they would without the merge. It keeps the earlier&#39;s reflector, source location and <code>meta</code>, falling back to the later&#39;s reflector when the earlier has none.</p><h4 id="config-settings-1" tabindex="-1">Config settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#config-settings-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Config settings&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li><strong>mode</strong> : <code>OpenApi\\Merge\\Mode|callable</code> · default: <code>Mode::Last</code><br> How two attributes with the same identity are treated where they overlap: a <code>Mode</code>, or a callable that receives the earlier and the later and returns one.</li></ul><h2 id="identity" tabindex="-1">Identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>What makes two attributes the same one depends on the collection they are in:</p><ul><li>anything in a <code>components</code> bucket: its component key</li><li>an operation: its path and method, or its webhook and method</li><li>a path item outside <code>components</code>: its path</li><li>a tag: its name</li></ul><p>Anything else has no identity and passes through. Servers and external documentation are positional, so duplicates there are legal and stay.</p><h2 id="modes" tabindex="-1">Modes <a class="header-anchor" href="#modes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Modes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>How a folding merger treats two attributes with the same identity where they overlap: a field, or a keyed child such as a parameter, that both set.</p><p>What only one of them sets is always taken. A merger that accepts a mode takes either a case or a callable that receives both and returns one, so the choice can depend on the pair, for example on a mark one producer left with <code>setMeta()</code>.</p><h3 id="mode-first" tabindex="-1"><code>Mode::First</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#mode-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`Mode::First`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Fold into the earlier attribute: on a field both set differently, the earlier value stays.</p><h3 id="mode-last" tabindex="-1"><code>Mode::Last</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#mode-last" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`Mode::Last`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Fold into the later attribute: on a field both set differently, the later value wins.</p><h3 id="mode-nonoverlapping" tabindex="-1"><code>Mode::NonOverlapping</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#mode-nonoverlapping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`Mode::NonOverlapping`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Take only what one of them has alone: a field or child both set is left as the earlier has it, and not folded into.</p>',40)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
